From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: btmtkbeif: keep the MCU running while the adapter is closed

With Wi-Fi holding connsys up, BGF does not come back from a power off.
The next power on times out on the power ack at 0x18000424, reloading the
module does not help, and only taking every radio off does. The first
close comes right after the first open, because the adapter has no address
yet, so Bluetooth did not work at all next to Wi-Fi.

The power off looks the same as the stock driver's and the cause is
unknown. On the device the next power on failed the same way when any one
step of the power off was left out (sleep protection, FUNC_EN, CPU reset,
A-die clock, semaphores, EMI requests, the radio bit of connsys), when the
MCU went to its firmware first, when the sleep protection was released
before the power on, and when bit 4 of 0x18000144, which differs after a
power off, was cleared.

So leave the MCU running. Shutdown turns the function off through WMT as
before, and if the firmware answers, close stops there. Open picks it up
again and setup turns the function back on. A real power off remains for
firmware that has failed, for a function off with no answer and for module
removal, and after those the failure above remains too.

The cost is an MCU that stays awake while the adapter is off, as it does
while the adapter is open. Given to its firmware in this state it does not
wake up ("no driver own: 00000004"), so a system sleep leaves it alone.
Neither the power nor a system sleep has been measured.

Seen on the device with Wi-Fi connected: closes and opens through
bluetoothctl and rfkill, each with function on and calibration status 0
and a scan that finds devices, with Wi-Fi still connected. A Wi-Fi chip
reset and a reload of the Wi-Fi module also work, with the adapter open
or closed.
---
 drivers/bluetooth/btmtkbeif.c | 45 +++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 44 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/bluetooth/btmtkbeif.c b/drivers/bluetooth/btmtkbeif.c
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/btmtkbeif.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/btmtkbeif.c
@@ -251,6 +251,9 @@ struct btmtkbeif_dev {
 	u32 tx_push;
 	bool fw_owned;
 
+	/* Closed, with the function off and the MCU running. See close. */
+	bool parked;
+
 	/* These belong to the thread of the software interrupt */
 	u32 rx_pop;
 	bool rx_more;
@@ -951,6 +954,23 @@ static int btmtkbeif_open(struct hci_dev *hdev)
 	struct btmtkbeif_dev *bdev = hci_get_drvdata(hdev);
 	int ret;
 
+	if (bdev->parked) {
+		bdev->parked = false;
+		bdev->opened = jiffies;
+
+		btmtkbeif_irqs_on(bdev);
+
+		ret = btmtkbeif_driver_own(bdev);
+		if (ret)
+			goto err_irqs_off;
+
+		set_bit(BTMTKBEIF_RUNNING, &bdev->flags);
+
+		bt_dev_info(hdev, "BGF: up again");
+
+		return 0;
+	}
+
 	ret = mtk_connsys_power_on(bdev->connsys, MTK_CONNSYS_RADIO_BT);
 	if (ret) {
 		bt_dev_err(hdev, "can't power connsys: %d", ret);
@@ -1015,6 +1035,19 @@ static int btmtkbeif_close(struct hci_dev *hdev)
 	bdev->rx_more = false;
 	kfree_skb(xchg(&bdev->evt_skb, NULL));
 
+	/*
+	 * Powered off while Wi-Fi holds connsys up, BGF does not come back. The
+	 * next power on times out on its power ack until every radio is off,
+	 * and nobody knows why. Leaving out any one step of the power off
+	 * changes nothing, and a parked MCU given to its firmware does not wake
+	 * up either. So it stays up with the function off unless it has failed.
+	 */
+	if (bdev->parked && !test_bit(BTMTKBEIF_DEAD, &bdev->flags)) {
+		bt_dev_info(hdev, "BGF: the MCU stays up");
+		return 0;
+	}
+	bdev->parked = false;
+
 	btmtkbeif_mcu_power_off(bdev);
 	mtk_connsys_power_off(bdev->connsys, MTK_CONNSYS_RADIO_BT);
 
@@ -1226,11 +1259,15 @@ static int btmtkbeif_setup(struct hci_dev *hdev)
 static int btmtkbeif_shutdown(struct hci_dev *hdev)
 {
 	struct btmtkbeif_dev *bdev = hci_get_drvdata(hdev);
+	int err;
 
 	if (test_bit(BTMTKBEIF_DEAD, &bdev->flags))
 		return 0;
 
-	return btmtkbeif_func_ctrl(hdev, 0);
+	err = btmtkbeif_func_ctrl(hdev, 0);
+	bdev->parked = !err;
+
+	return err;
 }
 
 /* Write a whole frame or nothing. The position in the ring is the firmware's to get wrong. */
@@ -1523,6 +1560,12 @@ static void btmtkbeif_remove(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	struct btmtkbeif_dev *bdev = platform_get_drvdata(pdev);
 
 	hci_unregister_dev(bdev->hdev);
+
+	if (bdev->parked) {
+		btmtkbeif_mcu_power_off(bdev);
+		mtk_connsys_power_off(bdev->connsys, MTK_CONNSYS_RADIO_BT);
+	}
+
 	hci_free_dev(bdev->hdev);
 }
 
